# 24. UV Structure in Compact Space

## 24.1 Introduction

Renormalization is conventionally regarded as a consequence of ultraviolet divergences arising from short-distance fluctuations. Since ultraviolet behavior is local, one might expect compactification to play no role in renormalization.

This expectation is only partially correct.

While leading ultraviolet singularities remain local and independent of global geometry, finite volume modifies the spectral organization of ultraviolet modes and introduces new geometric structures into renormalized observables.

The purpose of this chapter is to establish the ultraviolet foundations of finite-volume quantum field theory.

