# Conclusions of Part IV

Renormalization in compact space preserves the local ultraviolet structure of ordinary quantum field theory while fundamentally reorganizing its infrared behavior. Compactification introduces a physical infrared scale, converts divergent integrals into discrete spectral sums, and promotes geometry to a renormalization variable alongside energy scale. The resulting framework naturally leads to generalized renormalization-group flows on the two-scale space

[
(\mu,L),
]

with coupling constants becoming geometric functions rather than merely energy-dependent parameters.

The central outcome of Part IV is that finite-volume renormalization is not simply ordinary renormalization performed in a box. It is a broader geometric theory in which ultraviolet locality, infrared compactification, spectral regularization, and volume-dependent running are unified within a single framework. These results prepare the way for Part V, where finite-volume scattering theory, LSZ reduction, spectral reconstruction, and the emergence of S-matrix physics from compact spectra will be developed.
